<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vedas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vedas are the oldest scriptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 1500 BCE and transmitted orally for centuries. There are four, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>, each with hymns, ritual texts and later philosophical works including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. They remain the foundation of Hindu ritual and learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" width="320" height="132" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word veda means knowledge, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> root vid-, to know. The Vedas were composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> during the Vedic period, which spans the mid 2nd to mid 1st millennium BCE. The bulk of the Rigveda Samhita was composed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the north-west of the subcontinent, most likely between c. 1500 and 1200 BCE, though a wider range of c. 1700 to 1100 BCE has also been given. The other three Samhitas date from the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a>, c. 1200 to 900 BCE, and the commentarial and related texts, with the redaction of the Samhitas, from c. 1000 to 500 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> regard the Vedas as apauruṣeya, not of a man, and as revelations heard by ancient sages after intense meditation; they are śruti, what is heard, as against smṛti, what is remembered. The Vedic hymns themselves say that sages created them skilfully, as a carpenter builds a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>There are four Vedas: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>. The first three were the original division, the triple science of reciting hymns, performing sacrifices and chanting songs. Each Veda has four subdivisions: the Samhitas, which are mantras and benedictions; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, commentaries on rituals and sacrifices; the Aranyakas, on rituals and symbolic sacrifice; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, on meditation, philosophy and spiritual knowledge.</p><p>The Rigveda Samhita is the oldest surviving Indic text, with 1,028 hymns and 10,600 verses in ten books. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a> has 1,549 stanzas, almost all from the Rigveda except 75 mantras. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> is a compilation of prose offering formulas said by a priest during ritual actions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> has about 760 hymns, about 160 shared with the Rigveda, and was not counted as a Veda in the Vedic era but was accepted as one in the late 1st millennium BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Vedas were transmitted orally for centuries, from teacher to student or father to son, by elaborate mnemonic techniques. Only this tradition, embodied in a living teacher, is held to give the correct pronunciation and hidden meanings. The mantras are recited for their sound rather than their meaning. The texts were kept in separate schools, or śākhās; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> that survives belongs to a single well-preserved school, that of Śākalya, from Videha in what is now north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>.</p><p>The Vedangas, six auxiliary fields from phonetics to astronomy, arose because the language of the Vedas had become archaic. The Vedas and these fields were part of the curriculum at ancient universities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a>. Schools of Indian philosophy that accept the Vedas' authority are called orthodox, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and other śramaṇa traditions that did not are called heterodox.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vedas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the early Sangama kings the scholar Sayana and his collaborators produced commentaries on the <b>Vedas</b> that became standard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhism was one of several movements of the time that rejected the authority of the <b>Vedas</b> and the sacrificial ritual of the Brahmin priests.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oldest recorded root is the religion of the <b>Vedas</b>, brought into the north-west of the subcontinent by speakers of Indo-Aryan languages in the second millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The oldest form of the language, Vedic Sanskrit, is preserved in the <b>Vedas</b>, the sacred texts of the Brahmanical tradition.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vedic period, or Vedic age (c. 1500–c. 500 BCE), is the part of the late Bronze Age and early Iron Age in the northern Indian subcontinent when the Vedic literature, including the <b>Vedas</b> (c. 1500–900 BCE), was composed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>How far the movement set out to challenge caste, or the authority of the <b>Vedas</b> and of Sanskrit, remains one of the main questions that historians debate.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
